  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong to severe thunderstorms are likely\nTuesday, with some thunderstorm risk lingering into Wednesday.\n\nHigh pressure positioned over the northern Mid Atlantic/eastern\nGreat Lakes will provide below normal temperatures today. Most\nconvection should remain suppressed to the south until late\nafternoon or early evening. There may be a notable cumulus field\nhowever.\n\nAs the high departs this evening, a front stalled near the\nVirginia/North Carolina border will begin to lift northward,\nwith moisture and elevated instability increasing from south to\nnorth through the night. Therefore, some scattered showers and\nthunderstorms are possible, especially the second half of the\nnight. Central Virginia and the central Shenandoah Valley could\nsee activity earlier this evening, and there are some\nindications of higher rain totals from storms becoming locked\nalong the terrain.\n\nTuesday is the greatest day of concern this week. The warm front\nwill likely lift north of the area Tuesday morning, allowing a\nwarm and humid airmass to overspread the area. A seasonably\nstrong trough will be approaching from the west, providing\nforcing and at least 30 to 40 kt of shear. This combination\nprovides a favorable environment for severe thunderstorms, and\nit appears the typical afternoon and evening time frame will be\nfavored for the strongest storms. Some details remain\nunresolved, especially the effects of today's upstream\nconvection, any lingering local showers and clouds, and if there\nwill be any focal points for greater storm coverage.\nThunderstorms are also likely across the Ohio Valley, and the \nprogression of this activity could be in the mix as \nwell. Damaging winds will be the primary hazard, although large\nhail and a tornado or two are possible if supercells develop. \nRelatively speaking, the tornado threat is higher to the north \n(and north of the CWA) closer to the warm front. \n\nWith precipitable water values increasing (over 2 inches east of\nthe Blue Ridge), any storm mergers and training could result in\nlocalized flash flooding. There are many mesoscale details that\nneed to unfold, but additional rounds of showers and\nthunderstorms may continue through the night as the cold front\napproaches and a low level jet overspreads the area. While\nsevere weather steadily becomes less likely, I could see a\nlingering flash flood threat, as the ingredients may be in place\nfor slow moving or training heavy rainers.\n\nThere is some uncertainty with the cold frontal position by\nWednesday. With the upper trough overhead, additional showers\nand thunderstorms are likely, especially east of the Blue Ridge.\nWhile some additional strong to severe thunderstorms can't be\nruled out if things trend slower, it's beginning to appear the\nbest parameters for that to occur will be shunted toward\nsoutheast Virginia.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler than average with mainly dry conditions\nfor the end of the week as high pressure builds into the \nregion.\n\nAn upper level trough of low pressure will pivot its axis through \nthe region Thursday. This will allow for an area of high pressure \nto build in from the west late Thursday through early Sunday. \nThe result will be mainly dry conditions in most areas with \ntemperatures 5 to 10 degrees below average for the third week of\nJuly. It does look as if the high will begin to break down this\nweekend. The result will be an increase in humidity and daytime\ntemperatures approaching average for July by Sunday. Model\nspread increases during this time in regards when the next\nchance for rain could be.",
